1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Hello. Give an agent a shell, and eventually it'll reach for a command you never wanted it to run.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,000
You can ask it not to, but asking isn't a control.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:17,000
This lesson covers how to turn a hook into a real guardrail that stops the action cold. Let's dig in.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Three ideas frame the whole lesson. First, a guardrail.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:28,000
It's a control that denies or gates a dangerous action before it runs, and the key word is before.

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:33,000
A guardrail that catches things afterward isn't a guardrail. It's a report.

7
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:36,000
Second, the pre-tool use deny decision.

8
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:43,000
That's the specific hook move that blocks a pending tool call outright, so the action simply never happens.

9
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:46,000
It's the teeth behind the guardrail.

10
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Third, leased privilege. That means giving the agent only the tools and access it actually needs for its job.

11
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
The two work together. Leased privilege shrinks how much damage is even possible, and the guardrail

12
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,000
catches the dangerous cases

13
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:04,000
inside what's left.

14
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Narrow the surface first, then guard what remains.

15
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Let's make the guardrail pattern concrete, because it's smaller than you'd think. It has three moving parts.

16
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,000
You start with a matcher, which selects which tool calls your hook even looks at.

17
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:22,000
You might match on shell commands or on write operations,

18
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:30,000
so the hook stays focused instead of firing on everything. Then, inside the hook, you inspect the pending input.

19
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:36,000
You're looking for the specific thing you want to stop, say a recursive delete at the root of the file system.

20
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:42,000
When you find it, you return deny, and the call never executes. That's the entire shape.

21
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,000
Match the risky tool, inspect the call, deny the dangerous ones.

22
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:52,000
And because all of it happens before execution, the bad action is stopped, not just noticed.

23
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:00,000
This comparison is the heart of picking the right hook. Block before, detect after. With a pre-tool use deny,

24
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:06,000
you stop the action while the call is still pending. The dangerous command never touches your

25
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:08,000
system, because it never ran.

26
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:10,000
That's real prevention.

27
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:17,000
Now contrast a post-tool use hook. It only sees the result after the tool has already executed.

28
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:23,000
It can absolutely alert you or write a log entry, and that's genuinely useful for an audit trail,

29
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,000
but it can't undo what happened.

30
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:33,000
If your intent is to prevent harm, detection after the fact is the wrong tool, no matter how good the logging is.

31
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:40,000
So the rule writes itself. Prevention lives before execution, which means it lives in pre-tool use.

32
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Choosing the right lifecycle event is where guardrails quietly go wrong, and the fix is one question.

33
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Ask what you actually need to do at this point. If you need to prevent something, the event has to be pre-tool use,

34
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:59,000
because that's the only one that runs before the action. If you just need to observe or clean up afterward,

35
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:03,000
then a later event like post-tool use or stop is exactly right.

36
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,000
Here's the failure mode that makes this worth stressing.

37
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:13,000
Say you put a check for destructive commands in a post-tool use hook. It registers cleanly, and it even fires.

38
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:20,000
But it fires after the delete has already run, so your guardrail is really just a polite incident report.

39
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:23,000
Match the event to the moment, and the guardrail works.

40
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:26,000
Guardrails aren't only about blocking.

41
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:31,000
There are three deterministic uses worth keeping in mind, and they map to the decisions we covered.

42
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:38,000
The first is block. You deny a destructive command outright, a recursive delete, or a drop of a production table,

43
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:43,000
before it can execute. That's the deny decision doing its job.

44
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:51,000
The second is gate. Instead of a hard block, you use ask to route a high-stakes action to a human for approval.

45
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:56,000
The agent pauses, a person decides, and only then does it proceed.

46
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:00,000
The third is audit. You log every tool call deterministically,

47
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:05,000
so your record of what the agent did never depends on the model choosing to report it.

48
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:10,000
Block, gate, audit. Same mechanism, three different jobs.

49
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:17,000
Here's the property that makes hooks worth the trouble. A guardrail built as a hook can't be prompted away.

50
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:21,000
Think about why. The check lives in your code,

51
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:27,000
completely outside the model's reach. The model never even sees it, so there's nothing for a clever

52
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:28,000
input to talk around.

53
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:34,000
Compare that to a rule you write into the prompt, like never run destructive commands.

54
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:40,000
That instruction sits right there in the context, and the right adversarial input, or honestly,

55
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:46,000
just an unlucky phrasing, can get the model to step over it. The hook has no such weakness.

56
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:51,000
It fires the same way on every run, with no memory of past turns and no mood.

57
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:57,000
That's the whole reason serious guardrails live in hooks, not in prompt text.

58
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:03,000
So how do you actually wire a hookup? In the SDK, the primary path is code,

59
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:08,000
attaching your callback to options.hooks on your clod agent options.

60
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:13,000
The second surface is settings.json, where shell command hooks live.

61
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:19,000
Loaded when you enable setting sources. Either way, you name the lifecycle event

62
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:23,000
you're attaching to, like pre-tool use, and you give it a matcher.

63
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:27,000
The matcher is a filter that decides which tool calls the hook watches.

64
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:33,000
It might be an exact tool name, a pattern that catches a family of tools, or nothing at all,

65
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:40,000
which means match everything. One more mechanism worth knowing. A command style hook can return exit code, too,

66
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:45,000
and that's the deterministic signal that blocks the action at the shell level.

67
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:52,000
Register it, point the matcher at the right calls, then test it by trying the exact action you meant to block.

68
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:58,000
Let's put hooks in their place in the bigger picture, because they're one layer, not the whole story.

69
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:04,000
The foundation is least privilege. You give the agent only the tools and access it genuinely needs,

70
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:10,000
which shrinks the risk surface before any hook runs. If the agent can't reach production at all,

71
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:14,000
you don't need a guardrail for it. Then hooks sit on top,

72
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:20,000
guarding the dangerous actions that live inside the tools the agent legitimately does have.

73
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:22,000
Think of it as a stack.

74
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:28,000
Permissions set the baseline of what's even possible, and hooks add deterministic, case-by-case control

75
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:31,000
over the risky calls that remain.

76
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:36,000
When you layer them this way, a single missed instruction in a prompt doesn't turn into a real

77
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:38,000
incident, because the deterministic

78
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,000
controls underneath still hold.

79
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:47,000
Here's what to take away. When you build a guardrail, you're testing a pending action before it runs.

80
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:51,000
What to reach for is a pre-tool use deny or ask.

81
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:58,000
The trap is using a post-tool hook and trying to put your check after the fact, where the damage is already done.

82
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:03,000
That wraps up hooks as deterministic guardrails. I'll see you in the next one.

